---
import MayanPredictor from '@components/calculators/MayanPredictor.tsx';
import RelatedTools from '@theme/components/RelatedTools.astro';
import type { Dictionary } from '@i18n/translations';
import type { Locale } from '@i18n/utils';

interface Props { t: Dictionary; locale: Locale; }
const { t, locale } = Astro.props;
import GeoLeadLinks from '@theme/components/GeoLeadLinks.astro';
---

<div class="pt-10">
  <section class="section-shell grid items-start gap-10 lg:grid-cols-2">
    <div>
      <h1 class="font-display text-4xl font-extrabold leading-[1.06] text-plum-900 sm:text-5xl">
        Mayan <span class="bg-gradient-to-r from-girl-500 to-boy-500 bg-clip-text text-transparent">gender predictor</span>
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-plum-600">
        The pyramid people's answer to the Chinese chart: the Mayan method compares the <strong>parity</strong> (odd or even) of the
        mother's age at conception with the parity of the conception year — no lunar math required.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-6 rounded-3xl bg-cream-100 p-5 ring-1 ring-cream-200">
        <p class="font-display font-bold text-plum-800">🏛️ The rule, as widely circulated</p>
        <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm font-semibold leading-relaxed text-plum-600">{t.mayan.rule}</p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-plum-500">Source variants exist (some swap the verdict) — like all folklore, consistency isn't its strong suit. Estimated accuracy: ~50%.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="lg:sticky lg:top-24"><MayanPredictor client:idle strings={t.mayan} /></div>
  </section>
  <GeoLeadLinks />

  <section class="section-shell mt-14">
    <div class="prose-custom">
      <h2>Mayan vs. Chinese: which to trust?</h2>
      <p>
        Neither, medically — both converge to a coin flip when tested. But they disagree often, which makes running both a
        genuinely fun duel: if the <a href="/chinese-gender-predictor/">Chinese chart</a> and the Mayan parity rule agree, folklore wins
        the family table for a night; if they clash, the debate extends beautifully to delivery day.
      </p>
      <h2>Where the Mayans fit in the tradition</h2>
      <p>
        Unlike the chart (which claims Qing palace origins), the "Mayan predictor" as internet folklore is likely a 20th-century
        creation loosely inspired by the Maya vigesimal (base-20) love of numbers — no confirmed ancient manuscript survives.
        Its parity rule is deterministic, meaning it's honest about being arithmetic roulette.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <RelatedTools t={t} locale={locale} exclude="mayan-gender-predictor/" />
</div>
